  <h2>What a T&amp;M ticket is for</h2>
  <p>
    A time-and-materials ticket is the same-day record of extra work: the hours, the people, the
    materials and the equipment that went into something the GC directed but the contract did not
    include. It exists because memory is a bad witness. Three weeks later, when you price the change
    order, nobody will agree on how many hours the relocation took &mdash; unless a ticket signed by
    the superintendent that afternoon says so.
  </p>
  <p>
    The ticket has one job: prove cost. It does not change the contract, it does not set the price,
    and it does not authorize payment. Those happen on the
    <a href="/templates/change-order/">change order</a>, which the ticket supports as backup.
  </p>

  <h2>Field-by-field notes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Directed by and authorization.</strong> Name the person and tick how the direction came:
      written directive, verbal confirmed by your email, or verbal not confirmed. The third box exists
      so the ticket is honest about the weakest case &mdash; and so you send the email.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Reference and change order.</strong> Cite the drawing revision, RFI answer or directive
      number that made the work extra, and the change order number it will roll into. Those two lines
      are how the ticket, the change order and the
      <a href="/templates/aia-g702-g703/">pay application</a> stay traceable.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Labor.</strong> One line per person: name, classification, straight-time hours and rate,
      overtime hours and rate. Rates come from the subcontract or an agreed rate sheet. If someone worked
      two hours at time-and-a-half, the OT columns show it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Materials and equipment.</strong> Quantities and unit costs; hours or days and rates. Rentals
      brought in for the extra work belong here, as does a foreman&rsquo;s time spent on layout and supervision.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Notes and photos.</strong> Start and finish times, what the super witnessed, conditions
      found, and photo file references. The daily report number ties the ticket to the
      <a href="/templates/daily-report/">daily report</a> for the same day, where the same crew and hours
      should appear.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Signatures.</strong> Yours and the GC&rsquo;s, dated the day of the work. The acknowledgment
      language says the hours and materials were expended &mdash; not that the GC has agreed a price.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>A worked example</h2>
  <p>
    The preview shows the completed example that ships with the download. On Friday, September 11,
    2026, the GC&rsquo;s superintendent directs a mechanical subcontractor to relocate two supply
    duct drops that conflict with a revised light fixture layout (E-201 Rev. 3). The drops were
    installed to the mechanical drawings before the electrical revision came out, so the work is
    extra. Ticket T&amp;M-007 records the verbal direction at 7:15 AM and the confirming email at
    8:02 AM, then the labor: a sheet metal journeyman and an apprentice at eight straight-time hours
    each plus two hours of overtime at 1.5&times;, and one hour of the foreman&rsquo;s time for
    layout &mdash; $1,762.00. Three material lines add $276.00 and a day on a scissor lift $95.00,
    for a $2,133.00 subtotal; 15% overhead and profit brings the ticket total to $2,452.95. Six
    photos are referenced, the daily report number is noted, and both signatures are dated September
    11. The reference line says it will be priced as CO-005 &mdash; the ticket is the backup, the
    change order is the money.
  </p>

  <h2>T&amp;M ticket vs. change order vs. work order</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>T&amp;M ticket</strong> proves what directed extra work cost, signed the same day. A
    <strong>change order</strong> is the signed agreement on scope, price and time &mdash; the only document
    that changes what the contract pays. A
    <a href="/templates/work-order/">work order</a> authorizes and records a specific job, usually outside
    a contract: a service call, a warranty callback, a small standalone job. On a contract project, the
    sequence is ticket &rarr; change order &rarr; pay application. Skip the middle step and the ticket
    is just a well-documented loss.
  </p>
  <p>
    A signed ticket still has to become a <a href="/templates/change-order/">change order</a>
    before it bills on a <a href="/templates/aia-g702-g703/">pay application</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Using the Excel workbook</h2>
  <p>
    The T&amp;M Ticket sheet keeps the computed structure this template has always had:
    straight-time hours &times; rate, overtime hours &times; rate &times; the overtime multiplier
    (1.5 by default, editable), materials at quantity &times; unit cost, equipment at hours or days
    &times; rate, the section subtotals, overhead &amp; profit at the rate you enter, and the ticket
    total. The T&amp;M Log sheet takes one row per ticket with a status &mdash; awaiting change
    order, in a pending or approved change order, billed, disputed &mdash; and shows how many
    tickets and how much value are still waiting to become contract value. Every formula has been
    checked with real numbers.
  </p>

  <h2>Common mistakes this ticket is built to prevent</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Starting on a verbal OK with no confirming email.</li>
    <li>Writing the ticket at the end of the week from memory.</li>
    <li>Leaving off the foreman&rsquo;s supervision time and the lift rental.</li>
    <li>Using &ldquo;the usual rate&rdquo; instead of the contract rate.</li>
    <li>Getting the signature from someone who was not there.</li>
    <li>Filing the signed ticket and never converting it to a change order.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
